---Creating lightweight tags:
	* git tag v0.1 --This creates a tag named v0.1 pointing to the current commit (HEAD)
	* git log --oneline 
	* git tag v0.0.1 <commit-hash> --you get the hash from the above command 
	* git tag v0.0.1 29b1ace
	* git tag

---Creating Annotated Tags
-Annotated tags are stored as full objects in the Git database
-They contain the tagger's name, email, date, and a tagging message
-They can also be signed and verified with GNU Privacy Guard (GPG)

--To create an annotated tag, use the -a flag with git tag:
	* git tag -a v1.0 -m "First major release"
		--This creates an annotated tag named v1.0 with the message "First major release"
	* git show v1.0 
  --Annotated tags are recommended for public releases because they include much more information and can be verified

--If you want to search for tags matching a particular pattern, you can use the -l (or --list) option with a wildcard:
	* git tag -l "v1.*"
	* git tag -n 

---Checking Out Tags
	* git checkout v1.0 This will put your repository in a "detached HEAD" state at the commit that v1.0 points to
	* git checkout -b branch-name v1.0 make changes and create a new branch to retain these changes

---Deleting Tags
	* git tag -d v0.0.1


