	* git log
	* git log --oneline >> This shows each commit on a single line, with just the short commit hash and the commit message
	* git log --stat >> This will show you which files were changed in each commit and how many lines were added or removed
	* git log -p >> This shows the actual patch (the lines that were added and removed) for each commit
	* git log --pretty=format:"%h - %an, %ar : %s"
		--%h: abbreviated commit hash
		--%an: author name
		--%ar: author date, relative
		--%s: subject (commit message)
				--%H: full commit hash
				--%ad: author date
				--%cn: committer name
				--%ce: committer email
				--%cd: committer date
	* git log --since=1.week
	* git log --after="2023-06-01" --before="2023-06-30"
		--You can also use the --until option instead of --before, and --after is synonymous with --since
	* git log --grep="function" >> This shows all commits that affected the script.js file
	* git log --grep="function" -i >> This uses case insensative search with the -i flag
	* git log -S "console.log" >> This is called a "pickaxe" search -- It shows commits where the number of occurrences of "console.log" changed
	* git log -p --since=1.week -- script.js 
	* git shortlog -s -n >> This shows a list of authors, sorted by the number of commits, with the commit count next to each name
	* git log --author="Jane Doe" --pretty=tformat: --numstat | awk '{ add += $1; subs += $2; loc += $1 - $2 } END { printf "Added lines: %s, Removed lines: %s, Total lines: %s\n", add, subs, loc }'
		--Replace "Jane Doe" with the author's name you want to check
	* git log --pretty=format: --name-only | sort | uniq -c | sort -rg | head -10
	* git rev-list --count HEAD >> 